From 7fd8293816ca9028c79a1ca98bb439cfe272d523 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W1nkelm4nn <mrmarkap61@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 18:39:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] kk: Keep the AIR of archived pipelines and remove Metal's
 copies

Pipelines from the archives still need their functions, which Metal
compiles from MSL: Rise of the Tomb Raider's 28,000 libraries took
33 s on the compile threads, more than the archived pipelines. And
Metal's serializer leaves the working copy of each capture in the
app's cache, which every Wine process shares, as large as the archive,
and never removes it: 3.2 GB on the development Mac. Its metallib holds the
AIR of every function captured and loads in no time.

So each write moves the metallibs of the working copies that were
incomplete before it and are complete after next to the archive, as
NAME.K.air, and removes those copies; a new compiler removes the ones
nothing changed for 10 minutes, unless MESA_KK_KEEP_STALE_COPIES is
set. Entry points are named after a hash of the library's MSL, its
options and the macOS build, and a library whose entry point is in a
kept metallib comes from there instead of compiling.

Pipelines a game keeps building are written after 60 s too, and the
archives are written when the process exits, as Wine does when a game
ends. Wine kills the other threads first, maybe in the middle of a
build, so that write gives up after a second without the lock. A
pipeline is now counted before a write can take it, which wrote it
again. MESA_KK_COMPILE_STATS also prints the libraries from AIR and the
pipelines from archives.

Rise of the Tomb Raider with complete archives: libraries 1.8 instead
of 33 s, mean 27.7-28.1 instead of 26.6 fps, Geothermal Valley 23.7-24.4
instead of 21.7 fps, its 1 % low 17-18 instead of 14 fps.

Generated-by: LLM
